WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:02.961
[dramatic music]

2
00:00:05.047 --> 00:00:08.842
- [Tom Hanks] For almost four
years. American soldiers and

3
00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:11.887
Marines in the Pacific have
struggled across beaches.

4
00:00:11.887 --> 00:00:15.224
through jungles and atop
mountains to subdue a nation

5
00:00:15.224 --> 00:00:17.726
that refuses to surrender.

6
00:00:17.726 --> 00:00:20.479
The effort has been immense.

7
00:00:20.479 --> 00:00:21.813
More is required.

8
00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:25.651
In the spring of 1945.

9
00:00:25.651 --> 00:00:28.487
the United States will
start the final push

10
00:00:28.487 --> 00:00:31.281
to defeat the Japanese empire.

11
00:00:31.281 --> 00:00:32.573
[dramatic music]

12
00:00:32.573 --> 00:00:34.493
- All wars changed the world.

13
00:00:34.493 --> 00:00:36.328
but none of them
changed the world

14
00:00:36.328 --> 00:00:37.955
like the second World War did.

15
00:00:39.164 --> 00:00:41.792
- Japan's on the march.
Germany's on the march.

16
00:00:41.792 --> 00:00:43.168
[dramatic music continues]

17
00:00:43.168 --> 00:00:44.586
- No one can imagine
the nightmare

18
00:00:44.586 --> 00:00:45.921
they're about to unleash.

19
00:00:46.964 --> 00:00:49.841
The most destructive
war in human history.

20
00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:52.636
- Suddenly the world
is turned upside down

21
00:00:52.636 --> 00:00:54.304
and all hell is let loose.

22
00:00:54.304 --> 00:00:56.431
[dramatic music continues]

23
00:00:56.431 --> 00:00:59.685
- The West is stunned by
the speed of the advance.

24
00:00:59.685 --> 00:01:01.144
[dramatic music continues]

25
00:01:01.144 --> 00:01:03.689
- You get the allies
led by the big three.

26
00:01:03.689 --> 00:01:06.358
Roosevelt. Churchill. Stalin.

27
00:01:06.358 --> 00:01:11.363
men who are dealing with
immensely complicated questions.

28
00:01:11.363 --> 00:01:15.200
- It's the biggest military
operation of human history.

29
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.787
- The allies have to come
together. not just militarily

30
00:01:18.787 --> 00:01:20.080
but industrial scale.

31
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.249
It's a global perspective.

32
00:01:22.249 --> 00:01:24.042
- [Historian] They have
to fight in every climate

33
00:01:24.042 --> 00:01:27.504
from the Arctic to the
jungles of the Pacific.

34
00:01:27.504 --> 00:01:30.716
to the deserts of Africa
and the depths of the ocean.

35
00:01:30.716 --> 00:01:33.760
[dramatic music]

36
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.013
- But there was no
certainty of victory.

37
00:01:37.472 --> 00:01:40.225
It was going to be a
horrific bloodbath.

38
00:01:40.225 --> 00:01:42.394
- We see humans at
their absolute worst.

39
00:01:42.394 --> 00:01:44.604
how they treat
other human beings.

40
00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:46.356
and we see them at
their absolute best.

41
00:01:46.356 --> 00:01:49.151
willing to give their lives
that others might live.

42
00:01:49.151 --> 00:01:51.236
- World War II was a struggle

43
00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:55.824
in which there could be
one victor and vanquish.

44
00:01:55.824 --> 00:01:58.452
[dramatic music]

45
00:02:00.162 --> 00:02:02.789
[bomb exploding]

46
00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:05.584
[sirens wailing]

47
00:02:10.172 --> 00:02:13.717
[radio static crackling]

48
00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:19.014
- [Radio Host] With the
Rhine's West Bank secured.

49
00:02:19.014 --> 00:02:21.683
Britain expects other
crossings of Germany's river

50
00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:25.687
of Destiny after a law to
build up our own strength.

51
00:02:25.687 --> 00:02:28.440
and after that. the
end of the European war

52
00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.566
should not be far off.

53
00:02:29.566 --> 00:02:32.152
[dramatic music]

54
00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:35.489
- [Tom Hanks] In America
in the spring of 1945.

55
00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:39.534
there is hope that the war
in Europe is nearing its end.

56
00:02:39.534 --> 00:02:41.161
Husbands and fathers.

57
00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:45.624
boyfriends and brothers
might finally be coming home.

58
00:02:45.624 --> 00:02:48.877
- It is front and center
on the American mind.

59
00:02:48.877 --> 00:02:53.840
How quickly can we get to Berlin

60
00:02:54.716 --> 00:02:56.051
and get the soldiers home?

61
00:02:57.469 --> 00:02:59.262
- The public get this
sort of an attitude

62
00:02:59.262 --> 00:03:00.847
that you know. hey.
the war's almost over.

63
00:03:00.972 --> 00:03:04.643
They'll be coming home. but
it's almost like a marathon

64
00:03:04.643 --> 00:03:07.562
and you see the finish
line in the distance

65
00:03:07.562 --> 00:03:10.440
and so you know you
can give one last kick

66
00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.442
and then somebody says.

67
00:03:12.442 --> 00:03:14.903
"Actually there's another
three miles to go."

68
00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:18.407
- [Tom Hanks] Victory in
Europe seems within reach.

69
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.412
Victory over Tokyo remains
on the far horizon.

70
00:03:23.412 --> 00:03:27.082
[dramatic music]

71
00:03:27.082 --> 00:03:29.292
For months. hundreds of B-29s

72
00:03:29.292 --> 00:03:33.672
have been striking Japanese
cities to marginal effect.

73
00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:38.802
- The army air forces believed
that a sufficient amount

74
00:03:38.802 --> 00:03:41.763
of pinpoint bombing
from high altitude

75
00:03:41.763 --> 00:03:43.682
would take out Japanese industry

76
00:03:43.682 --> 00:03:47.102
and make it impossible for
them to continue the war.

77
00:03:47.102 --> 00:03:48.145
It didn't work.

78
00:03:48.145 --> 00:03:50.522
[dramatic music continues]

79
00:03:50.522 --> 00:03:53.150
Bombing from 30.000 feet.
the winds were too strong.

80
00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:54.943
blew the bombs all
over the place.

81
00:03:56.445 --> 00:03:59.322
- [Tom Hanks] Under the command
of General Curtis LeMay.

82
00:03:59.322 --> 00:04:01.867
the Air Force
alters its tactics.

83
00:04:04.995 --> 00:04:07.956
[fast-paced music]

84
00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:12.169
[airplane roaring]

85
00:04:12.169 --> 00:04:14.880
On March 9th. 1945.

86
00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.134
LeMay's B-29s will attack
Tokyo from only 5.000 feet

87
00:04:19.134 --> 00:04:20.760
using a new weapon.

88
00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:23.680
Bombs filled with napalm.

89
00:04:25.015 --> 00:04:28.226
- Napalm is this gel
combined with gasoline

90
00:04:28.226 --> 00:04:30.854
that will stick to things
and then it catches fire

91
00:04:30.854 --> 00:04:33.273
and it makes it very.
very hard to put out.

92
00:04:33.273 --> 00:04:35.442
It's for starting
fires that are designed

93
00:04:35.442 --> 00:04:37.611
to just go out of control.

94
00:04:37.611 --> 00:04:39.654
- And the reason it's
gonna be so destructive

95
00:04:39.654 --> 00:04:43.325
is that Tokyo is mostly
made of wood and paper

96
00:04:43.325 --> 00:04:45.952
and this is just
going to explode.

97
00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:47.871
It'll be incredibly imflamable.

98
00:04:49.206 --> 00:04:52.000
- [Craig] The argument was that
if you destroy the housing units

99
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.961
of the Japanese workers. they
wouldn't be able to go to work

100
00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:56.671
and build the planes
and the tanks.

101
00:04:56.671 --> 00:04:58.840
They needed to
continue the conflict.

102
00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.593
[dramatic music]

103
00:05:01.593 --> 00:05:04.304
But the outcome was
absolutely horrific.

104
00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:05.972
[dramatic music]

105
00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:08.767
[bombs whirring]

106
00:05:11.603 --> 00:05:14.356
[fire crackling]

107
00:05:15.565 --> 00:05:18.109
- [Historian] It is just
complete devastation in Tokyo.

108
00:05:18.109 --> 00:05:20.445
[soft music]

109
00:05:20.445 --> 00:05:22.948
A hundred thousand
people were killed.

110
00:05:22.948 --> 00:05:24.866
[soft music continues]

111
00:05:24.866 --> 00:05:27.869
- [Saul] We think of Dresden as
being the worst single moment

112
00:05:27.869 --> 00:05:29.913
in the European
Theater of Operations.

113
00:05:29.913 --> 00:05:33.917
The fire storm at Dresden.
25.000 were lost there.

114
00:05:33.917 --> 00:05:36.920
but in Tokyo there's a
hundred thousand killed

115
00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:38.672
in a single operation.

116
00:05:38.672 --> 00:05:41.800
The whole city
was a sea of fire.

117
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.677
[soft music]

118
00:05:43.677 --> 00:05:46.888
- [Christopher] It was so hot
that canals start to boil

119
00:05:46.888 --> 00:05:49.391
and the fires eat up
so much of the oxygen

120
00:05:49.391 --> 00:05:51.518
that a lot of the people
who die asphyxiate

121
00:05:51.518 --> 00:05:53.603
because there's no oxygen left.

122
00:05:53.603 --> 00:05:56.731
A huge proportion of the city
is simply gone in one night.

123
00:05:56.731 --> 00:05:58.483
[soft music]

124
00:05:58.483 --> 00:06:01.278
- [Tom Hanks] The
destruction is immense.

125
00:06:01.278 --> 00:06:04.906
16 square miles of
Tokyo are obliterated.

126
00:06:06.116 --> 00:06:07.534
After this one raid.

127
00:06:07.534 --> 00:06:10.161
a million Japanese no
longer have a home.

128
00:06:11.288 --> 00:06:12.998
- It's unprecedented.

129
00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:18.920
General LeMay later I recall
that he would've been tried

130
00:06:20.171 --> 00:06:25.135
as a war criminal if United
States lost in the war.

131
00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:28.847
[soft music]

132
00:06:30.974 --> 00:06:33.310
- [Tom Hanks] To
survey the damage.

133
00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.521
Emperor Hirohito
emerges from his bunker

134
00:06:36.521 --> 00:06:38.773
beneath the Imperial Palace.

135
00:06:40.483 --> 00:06:44.696
- Emperor Hirohito has
been pretty isolated

136
00:06:44.696 --> 00:06:46.448
throughout the war.

137
00:06:46.448 --> 00:06:51.036
but he does have a sense that
the war is not exactly going

138
00:06:51.036 --> 00:06:54.122
as his generals and
admirals are telling him.

139
00:06:54.122 --> 00:06:57.042
So when the fire bombing
in Tokyo happened.

140
00:06:57.042 --> 00:06:59.711
that only intensifies his desire

141
00:06:59.711 --> 00:07:01.921
to start looking for
ways to end the war.

142
00:07:03.757 --> 00:07:05.592
- [Christopher] For Emperor
Hirohito the big concern

143
00:07:05.592 --> 00:07:09.346
is not what might happen to
the ordinary Japanese people.

144
00:07:09.346 --> 00:07:11.848
but if the Americans make
it onto Japanese soil.

145
00:07:11.848 --> 00:07:13.642
what might happen to him?

146
00:07:13.642 --> 00:07:15.685
What might happen
to the imperial ine

147
00:07:15.685 --> 00:07:18.521
that goes back
centuries in Japan?

148
00:07:18.521 --> 00:07:22.317
He says. "I can't
possibly face my ancestors

149
00:07:22.317 --> 00:07:24.152
having sacrificed the throne."

150
00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:27.322
- [Tom Hanks] The emperor
of Japan is considered

151
00:07:27.322 --> 00:07:31.576
to be divine with immense
authority over his people.

152
00:07:31.576 --> 00:07:35.038
But Hirohito rarely exerts
that authority in political

153
00:07:35.038 --> 00:07:36.998
or military matters.

154
00:07:36.998 --> 00:07:39.250
- There's the committee
for the supreme direction

155
00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:42.671
of the war. which is six people.
two people from the army.

156
00:07:42.671 --> 00:07:44.923
two people from the
Navy. the prime minister

157
00:07:44.923 --> 00:07:46.091
and the foreign minister.

158
00:07:46.091 --> 00:07:47.842
Those six are the only six

159
00:07:47.842 --> 00:07:50.053
that had any real
say within Japan.

160
00:07:51.805 --> 00:07:53.765
- The civilian
leaders understood they

161
00:07:53.765 --> 00:07:56.434
couldn't win the war. but the
military are determined to

162
00:07:56.434 --> 00:07:57.936
fight on.

163
00:07:58.144 --> 00:08:00.397
- Japanese military leaders
have lived their whole life

164
00:08:00.397 --> 00:08:02.107
under a professional code.

165
00:08:02.107 --> 00:08:06.236
That surrender is
so dishonorable you

166
00:08:06.236 --> 00:08:07.987
would rather die. no matter
what the suffering.

167
00:08:07.987 --> 00:08:11.282
the warrior does not surrender.

168
00:08:11.282 --> 00:08:12.992
- [Jon] For all the
firebombing.

169
00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:16.413
for all the destruction
already rained on Japan.

170
00:08:16.413 --> 00:08:18.081
there's no sign of surrender.

171
00:08:18.081 --> 00:08:20.667
- [Craig] The American high
command now believes

172
00:08:20.667 --> 00:08:24.713
that the Japanese would not
surrender short of an invasion

173
00:08:24.713 --> 00:08:27.841
and occupation of the
Japanese home islands.

174
00:08:27.841 --> 00:08:30.427
[dramatic music]

175
00:08:30.427 --> 00:08:32.804
- [Tom Hanks] The United
States is gathering its forces

176
00:08:32.804 --> 00:08:36.433
for the next phase of its
assault on Imperial Japan.

177
00:08:36.433 --> 00:08:40.770
[boat whirring and
dramatic music]

178
00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.731
The capture of Okinawa.

179
00:08:42.731 --> 00:08:46.234
the largest island
on the Ryukyu chain.

180
00:08:46.234 --> 00:08:50.113
- Okinawa is just 400
miles south of Kyushu.

181
00:08:50.113 --> 00:08:52.866
which is the most southerly
of the Japanese home islands.

182
00:08:52.866 --> 00:08:56.786
and therefore can be used
to launch an invasion fleet.

183
00:08:56.786 --> 00:08:59.998
So Okinawa is absolutely
vital to the Americans.

184
00:08:59.998 --> 00:09:03.626
[dramatic music continues]

185
00:09:07.297 --> 00:09:09.132
- [Craig] Okinawa would be
defended

186
00:09:09.132 --> 00:09:12.844
by about a hundred thousand
Japanese frontline troops

187
00:09:12.844 --> 00:09:16.723
and also native
Okinawan militia forces.

188
00:09:16.723 --> 00:09:20.769
They know that the Americans
have overwhelming superiority

189
00:09:20.769 --> 00:09:23.605
in the air and at sea.

190
00:09:23.605 --> 00:09:25.482
[dramatic music continues]

191
00:09:25.482 --> 00:09:28.401
- [Christopher] The plan for
the defense of Okinawa

192
00:09:28.401 --> 00:09:30.403
is don't go for a Banzai charge.

193
00:09:30.403 --> 00:09:33.698
Instead. they will prepare
these defensive fortifications.

194
00:09:33.698 --> 00:09:38.536
let the Americans come to them
and take them out that way.

195
00:09:38.536 --> 00:09:40.330
[dramatic music continues]

196
00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:43.124
- [Tom Hanks] Using native
Okinawans as forced labor.

197
00:09:44.626 --> 00:09:48.088
the Japanese build a network
of bunkers and tunnels.

198
00:09:48.088 --> 00:09:51.174
- [Craig] And the idea was kill
four. five Americans

199
00:09:51.174 --> 00:09:53.009
before you are killed yourself.

200
00:09:54.636 --> 00:09:57.722
The Japanese overall strategy
is to make the Americans pay

201
00:09:57.722 --> 00:10:00.391
so much for Okinawa
that an invasion

202
00:10:00.391 --> 00:10:03.561
of the Japanese home islands
would be unthinkable.

203
00:10:03.561 --> 00:10:06.481
[dramatic music]

204
00:10:08.775 --> 00:10:12.320
- [Saul] The garrison on Okinawa
is only half the Japanese

205
00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:13.613
strategy for
defending the island.

206
00:10:13.822 --> 00:10:16.991
The other element of the
plan is to use kamikaze.

207
00:10:16.991 --> 00:10:20.370
but in far greater numbers
than ever been used before.

208
00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:21.830
- The Japanese recognize

209
00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:23.206
that when the Americans conduct

210
00:10:23.206 --> 00:10:25.083
these amphibious
landing operations.

211
00:10:25.083 --> 00:10:27.710
the ships offshore
are the lifeline

212
00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:30.880
that's keeping the battalions
ashore in the fight.

213
00:10:32.173 --> 00:10:35.510
So they came up with this
idea of these organized waves

214
00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:36.761
of kamikaze attacks.

215
00:10:38.763 --> 00:10:43.518
- [Dan] And if you launch 300
kamikaze at the American fleet

216
00:10:43.518 --> 00:10:46.521
and you take out seven
aircraft carriers or something.

217
00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.233
that is a war changing thing.

218
00:10:50.233 --> 00:10:53.027
[bombs exploding]

219
00:10:56.156 --> 00:10:57.866
- [Tom Hanks] The
Battle of Okinawa starts

220
00:10:57.866 --> 00:11:00.410
with the largest invasion
of the war in the Pacific.

221
00:11:02.036 --> 00:11:05.957
A massive effort combining
naval. air and ground support.

222
00:11:07.125 --> 00:11:09.294
60.000 soldiers and
marines are bound

223
00:11:09.294 --> 00:11:11.212
for Okinawa's western beaches.

224
00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:15.842
The invasion force will
land on Hagushi Bay.

225
00:11:15.842 --> 00:11:17.760
then the Marines
will sweep north

226
00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:21.556
and the army south to take
control of the entire island.

227
00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.268
[guns firing]

228
00:11:26.436 --> 00:11:28.688
- [Saul] The biggest danger in
an amphibious operation

229
00:11:28.688 --> 00:11:32.692
is the actual landing because
you are incredibly vulnerable.

230
00:11:32.692 --> 00:11:34.611
You are out in the open.

231
00:11:34.611 --> 00:11:36.696
The combat troops
going up first.

232
00:11:36.696 --> 00:11:39.908
Both marine and army have
been told to expect up

233
00:11:39.908 --> 00:11:41.451
to 80% casualties.

234
00:11:41.451 --> 00:11:43.828
[dramatic music]

235
00:11:43.828 --> 00:11:46.831
So if you've drawn the
unlucky straw of being one

236
00:11:46.831 --> 00:11:48.875
of the first units. you're
pretty convinced not many

237
00:11:48.875 --> 00:11:50.793
of you're gonna survive.

238
00:11:50.793 --> 00:11:52.837
[dramatic music continues]

239
00:11:52.837 --> 00:11:54.797
They to the beaches
start walking up it.

240
00:11:54.797 --> 00:11:57.592
body's tense waiting
for the bullets to come

241
00:11:58.635 --> 00:12:00.303
and there is nothing.

242
00:12:00.303 --> 00:12:03.723
[dramatic music]

243
00:12:03.723 --> 00:12:07.310
- [Martin] They're experiencing
virtually no opposition.

244
00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:08.603
And so there was a
thought that hey.

245
00:12:08.603 --> 00:12:10.063
maybe Okinawa's
not gonna turn out

246
00:12:10.063 --> 00:12:12.190
to be a big bloodbath after all.

247
00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:15.235
[fast-paced music]

248
00:12:15.235 --> 00:12:16.527
- [Saul] The first few days go
very well for the

249
00:12:16.527 --> 00:12:17.987
landing forces.

250
00:12:17.987 --> 00:12:20.365
They cut across all the
way to the other side

251
00:12:20.365 --> 00:12:23.201
of the island against
minimal opposition.

252
00:12:23.201 --> 00:12:24.369
[fast-paced music continues]

253
00:12:24.577 --> 00:12:26.913
It's looking too good to
be true at this stage.

254
00:12:26.913 --> 00:12:29.791
[dramatic music]

255
00:12:33.544 --> 00:12:37.465
- [Yuki] Military leaders
believed that Japan must fight

256
00:12:37.465 --> 00:12:40.802
to protect the honor
of the country.

257
00:12:40.802 --> 00:12:45.556
And these kamikaze pilots
are the chosen ones

258
00:12:45.556 --> 00:12:48.893
to go to this divine mission
to protect their homeland.

259
00:12:50.353 --> 00:12:51.604
They really believe that

260
00:12:51.604 --> 00:12:54.148
that would turn the
tide of the war.

261
00:12:55.525 --> 00:12:59.320
- [Craig] The pilots are young.
many of them are teenagers.

262
00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:00.863
they're ill trained.

263
00:13:00.863 --> 00:13:02.282
They don't even have enough fuel

264
00:13:02.282 --> 00:13:04.993
to come back from this
flight if they wanted to.

265
00:13:04.993 --> 00:13:06.327
They know they're going to die

266
00:13:08.413 --> 00:13:13.584
and so when they get to the
first American ship they see.

267
00:13:15.878 --> 00:13:17.005
they attack.

268
00:13:20.341 --> 00:13:22.218
[dramatic music]

269
00:13:25.138 --> 00:13:26.681
- [Tom Hanks] For
the first five days

270
00:13:26.681 --> 00:13:29.726
of the invasion of Okinawa.
there's no opposition

271
00:13:29.726 --> 00:13:32.645
to the massive US fleet
surrounding the island.

272
00:13:32.645 --> 00:13:35.440
[dramatic music]

273
00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:38.192
But just after
noon on April 6th.

274
00:13:38.192 --> 00:13:40.695
the Japanese arrive in force.

275
00:13:40.695 --> 00:13:45.867
[dramatic music]
[signals beeping]

276
00:13:47.285 --> 00:13:48.911
- Imagine we're on
the bridge of one

277
00:13:48.911 --> 00:13:52.832
of these destroyers 80
miles north of Okinawa.

278
00:13:52.832 --> 00:13:55.251
The kamikaze come from
starboard. from port.

279
00:13:55.251 --> 00:13:56.919
from high. from low.

280
00:13:56.919 --> 00:14:01.049
[dramatic music and
planes whirring]

281
00:14:01.049 --> 00:14:03.092
So you feel like
you're in a boxing ring

282
00:14:03.092 --> 00:14:05.553
with about five other
boxers attacking you

283
00:14:05.553 --> 00:14:06.846
from every direction.

284
00:14:06.846 --> 00:14:11.809
[planes whirring and
dramatic music continues]

285
00:14:12.894 --> 00:14:15.605
- Hundreds of kamikaze
at a time thrown

286
00:14:15.605 --> 00:14:18.733
against the invading fleet.

287
00:14:18.733 --> 00:14:21.444
They thought with the sheer
numbers they could take out

288
00:14:21.444 --> 00:14:24.655
the capital ships
that's battleships

289
00:14:24.655 --> 00:14:25.823
and aircraft carriers and drive
off the US fleet

290
00:14:25.823 --> 00:14:27.366
in its entirety.

291
00:14:27.366 --> 00:14:29.994
[bomb exploding]

292
00:14:29.994 --> 00:14:32.914
- [Craig] The kamikaze don't
really have much success

293
00:14:32.914 --> 00:14:35.041
against aircraft carriers.

294
00:14:35.041 --> 00:14:37.960
[bombs whirring]

295
00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:43.841
but 300 plus ships are
targeted by the kamikazes.

296
00:14:44.801 --> 00:14:46.969
More than 36 of them sink.

297
00:14:46.969 --> 00:14:49.680
[dramatic music]

298
00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:53.476
It is more devastating than
any conventional bombing attack

299
00:14:53.476 --> 00:14:58.481
has been in the war so
far. but it's not enough.

300
00:14:59.357 --> 00:15:01.150
- What the kamikaze tells me is

301
00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:04.278
that the Japanese
knew the war was lost.

302
00:15:04.278 --> 00:15:06.280
at least by conventional means.

303
00:15:07.573 --> 00:15:09.325
There's nothing more
dangerous in warfare

304
00:15:09.325 --> 00:15:11.244
than a cornered enemy.

305
00:15:11.244 --> 00:15:14.122
The Japanese are gonna
go down in flames

306
00:15:14.122 --> 00:15:17.041
but will never surrender. never.

307
00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.669
- [Tom Hanks] For the US Navy.

308
00:15:19.669 --> 00:15:22.922
the battle against the
kamikaze at Okinawa is bloodier

309
00:15:22.922 --> 00:15:27.051
than any previous confrontation
of the entire Pacific War.

310
00:15:29.345 --> 00:15:31.722
And American ground
forces are now facing

311
00:15:31.722 --> 00:15:33.683
increasing resistance as well.

312
00:15:35.393 --> 00:15:38.563
- [Martin] The force that had
landed on the beaches is pushing

313
00:15:38.563 --> 00:15:41.232
south toward a place
called Kakazu Ridge

314
00:15:41.232 --> 00:15:44.193
where they encounter the first
line of Japanese defenses.

315
00:15:44.193 --> 00:15:45.361
[dramatic music]

316
00:15:45.361 --> 00:15:48.573
[bomb exploding]

317
00:15:48.573 --> 00:15:50.533
- [Saul] It's the first
time the Americans

318
00:15:50.533 --> 00:15:53.411
meet serious opposition
and it stops them dead.

319
00:15:53.411 --> 00:15:57.081
[dramatic music continues]

320
00:15:59.417 --> 00:16:01.711
The Japanese have created
a defensive system

321
00:16:01.711 --> 00:16:04.881
that goes right across the
whole width of the island.

322
00:16:04.881 --> 00:16:06.424
so they can't be out flanked.

323
00:16:07.675 --> 00:16:09.802
- [Tom Hanks] American
troops endure days

324
00:16:09.802 --> 00:16:11.721
of nonstop enemy fire.

325
00:16:11.721 --> 00:16:14.682
[guns shooting]

326
00:16:14.682 --> 00:16:18.561
- [Saul] This is attritional.
grizzly. meat grinding fighting.

327
00:16:20.396 --> 00:16:22.982
and that's just the
first line of defense

328
00:16:22.982 --> 00:16:25.401
there are gonna be
many more beyond this.

329
00:16:26.903 --> 00:16:29.322
What we thought was gonna be
a cakewalk is anything but.

330
00:16:29.322 --> 00:16:32.241
[dramatic music]

331
00:16:33.784 --> 00:16:35.536
- [Tom Hanks] April 12th.

332
00:16:35.536 --> 00:16:39.123
The American people learn
Franklin Delano Roosevelt.

333
00:16:39.123 --> 00:16:42.710
the man they elected
four times is dead.

334
00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:45.046
[soft music]

335
00:16:46.589 --> 00:16:49.884
Vice president Harry S.
Truman. a former judge

336
00:16:49.884 --> 00:16:53.763
and senator from Missouri
is sworn in hours later.

337
00:16:55.389 --> 00:16:57.934
- Roosevelt had led the country
through the Great Depression

338
00:16:57.934 --> 00:16:59.435
and through the war.

339
00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:04.648
In the 82 days that Truman
had been vice president.

340
00:17:04.648 --> 00:17:07.401
he met with Roosevelt
all of twice.

341
00:17:07.401 --> 00:17:09.862
He really knows nothing
about what's going on

342
00:17:09.862 --> 00:17:11.446
with the war effort.

343
00:17:11.446 --> 00:17:13.241
- He didn't think
he was up to the job

344
00:17:13.241 --> 00:17:16.243
and there are people who
would say he was right.

345
00:17:16.243 --> 00:17:19.079
- [Tom Hanks] The new president
is immediately confronted

346
00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:20.748
with enormous challenges.

347
00:17:22.124 --> 00:17:24.835
- [Jonathan] It's important for
the wartime president to

348
00:17:24.835 --> 00:17:26.671
demonstrate that he has a plan
for bringing this war

349
00:17:26.671 --> 00:17:30.007
to a successful conclusion
and as rapidly as possible.

350
00:17:30.007 --> 00:17:31.926
It's clear to the
American population

351
00:17:31.926 --> 00:17:34.720
that Germany is
on its last legs.

352
00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:37.306
but Japan is still
fighting hard.

353
00:17:37.306 --> 00:17:40.268
- The question really is. how
many American lives am I going

354
00:17:40.268 --> 00:17:42.562
to lose to end this war?

355
00:17:42.562 --> 00:17:45.147
That's the whole ball
game for Harry Truman.

356
00:17:45.147 --> 00:17:48.025
[dramatic music]

357
00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:53.114
- [Tom Hanks] Truman meets
with Secretary of War

358
00:17:53.114 --> 00:17:57.243
Henry Stimson and Army
General Leslie Groves.

359
00:17:57.243 --> 00:17:59.495
The agenda is classified.

360
00:17:59.495 --> 00:18:01.622
- [A.J.] They hand Truman a
document.

361
00:18:02.456 --> 00:18:04.041
Truman opens it up and reads it

362
00:18:04.041 --> 00:18:06.627
and the memo begins with
these dramatic words.

363
00:18:06.627 --> 00:18:08.004
"Within four months.

364
00:18:08.004 --> 00:18:10.965
we shall in all
probability have completed

365
00:18:10.965 --> 00:18:14.760
the most terrible weapon
ever known in human history.

366
00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:18.681
one bomb which can
destroy a whole city."

367
00:18:18.681 --> 00:18:20.474
[dramatic music]

368
00:18:20.474 --> 00:18:22.602
- [Tom Hanks] The
Manhattan Project.

369
00:18:22.602 --> 00:18:25.187
the top secret American program

370
00:18:25.187 --> 00:18:28.190
to develop the world's
first atomic bombs.

371
00:18:29.525 --> 00:18:33.487
For three years. thousands
of scientists and engineers

372
00:18:33.487 --> 00:18:36.365
have worked on the most
powerful weapon in history.

373
00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:39.744
It is set to be
tested midsummer.

374
00:18:41.203 --> 00:18:44.206
- [Robert] Truman and his
commanders are ready to pay any

375
00:18:44.206 --> 00:18:46.125
price to bring
this war to an end.

376
00:18:46.125 --> 00:18:48.586
But an invasion of the
Japanese home islands

377
00:18:48.586 --> 00:18:51.255
will be bloody on an
unimaginable scale.

378
00:18:51.255 --> 00:18:54.175
Higher losses than
any previous campaign

379
00:18:54.175 --> 00:18:57.762
either in the Pacific War
or in the war in Europe.

380
00:18:58.596 --> 00:19:00.681
- The Manhattan project.

381
00:19:00.681 --> 00:19:04.560
the attempt to develop
atomic weapons.

382
00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:05.436
If that proves to be successful.

383
00:19:05.436 --> 00:19:07.146
If that proves to be successful.

384
00:19:07.146 --> 00:19:11.192
if those tests prove out.
then that is an alternative.

385
00:19:14.362 --> 00:19:17.365
[bells ringing and
fast-paced music]

386
00:19:17.365 --> 00:19:19.241
- [Tom Hanks] The war
in Europe is over.

387
00:19:20.409 --> 00:19:22.286
The Nazi Third Reich
has been destroyed.

388
00:19:22.286 --> 00:19:26.290
[fast-paced music continues]

389
00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:28.376
[whistle hissing]

390
00:19:28.376 --> 00:19:31.003
But for war weary
America and its allies.

391
00:19:32.713 --> 00:19:34.715
the war in the Pacific continues

392
00:19:35.883 --> 00:19:38.678
and many of the men who
helped defeat Germany

393
00:19:38.678 --> 00:19:42.306
may now be required
to fight Japan.

394
00:19:42.306 --> 00:19:44.809
- At this point. people in
the military knew that a lot

395
00:19:44.809 --> 00:19:47.687
of these units are gonna
need to be transferred.

396
00:19:47.687 --> 00:19:49.647
Just because the war
in Europe is over.

397
00:19:49.647 --> 00:19:51.190
don't get excited.

398
00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:53.526
We're gonna need those
troops in the Pacific.

399
00:19:53.526 --> 00:19:56.195
[guns firing]

400
00:19:58.906 --> 00:20:01.867
- [Tom Hanks] After two
months of ceaseless combat.

401
00:20:01.867 --> 00:20:03.744
Marine and Army units break

402
00:20:03.744 --> 00:20:07.081
through the main Japanese
defensive line on Okinawa.

403
00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:11.001
Japanese troops are
forced to retreat

404
00:20:11.001 --> 00:20:14.922
and attempt a last stand
on the Kiyan Peninsula.

405
00:20:14.922 --> 00:20:17.007
- [Christopher ] By this point.
the Japanese soldiers on Okinawa

406
00:20:17.007 --> 00:20:18.926
are low on ammunition.

407
00:20:18.926 --> 00:20:22.513
they are starving.
they're exhausted.

408
00:20:22.513 --> 00:20:24.682
but they realize that the
next step for the allies

409
00:20:24.682 --> 00:20:27.184
after Okinawa is
the home islands.

410
00:20:27.184 --> 00:20:29.562
They are all that stand
between the allies

411
00:20:29.562 --> 00:20:31.772
and their families. their homes.

412
00:20:31.772 --> 00:20:34.567
They are the last
line of defense

413
00:20:34.567 --> 00:20:36.318
and everyone is
depending on them.

414
00:20:36.318 --> 00:20:39.113
[dramatic music]

415
00:20:42.283 --> 00:20:44.869
- [Martin] Although the
Americans have gained ground.

416
00:20:44.869 --> 00:20:47.913
[bombs exploding]

417
00:20:47.913 --> 00:20:49.957
the more you push
and the more terrain

418
00:20:49.957 --> 00:20:52.376
that has been captured.
the more dangerous

419
00:20:52.376 --> 00:20:54.211
and costly the
battle is becoming.

420
00:20:54.211 --> 00:20:56.422
[dramatic music continues]

421
00:20:56.422 --> 00:20:58.632
- But the Americans know
we're making progress.

422
00:20:58.632 --> 00:21:01.010
If war is a clash of wills.

423
00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:03.429
the United States is a nation
willing to fight it out

424
00:21:03.429 --> 00:21:04.513
to the end.

425
00:21:04.513 --> 00:21:07.641
[dramatic music]

426
00:21:07.641 --> 00:21:09.769
- [Tom Hanks] Organized
resistance on Okinawa

427
00:21:09.769 --> 00:21:11.479
ends On June 22nd.

428
00:21:12.605 --> 00:21:15.107
The Japanese Army's
commanding general

429
00:21:15.107 --> 00:21:18.444
and his chief of staff
both commit suicide

430
00:21:18.444 --> 00:21:20.070
by ritual seppuku.

431
00:21:21.363 --> 00:21:24.074
- [Saul] A hundred thousand
Japanese servicemen are killed.

432
00:21:25.284 --> 00:21:26.827
On the American side.

433
00:21:26.827 --> 00:21:28.913
the casualties are 50.000
killed and wounded.

434
00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:31.248
These are the worst

435
00:21:31.457 --> 00:21:34.084
of battle casualties in the
Pacific War for the Americans.

436
00:21:36.128 --> 00:21:39.173
worse than all of that are the
number of civilians killed.

437
00:21:40.424 --> 00:21:43.594
Up to 125.000. which is a third

438
00:21:43.594 --> 00:21:45.763
of the population
caught in the crossfire.

439
00:21:46.931 --> 00:21:48.390
- [Col. Douglas] This is just
Okinawa.

440
00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:50.851
What happens when we get
to their home islands?

441
00:21:50.851 --> 00:21:53.354
The cost is gonna be enormous.

442
00:21:54.188 --> 00:21:55.481
This war ain't over.

443
00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:58.108
It's not even close
to being over.

444
00:21:58.108 --> 00:22:00.986
[dramatic music]

445
00:22:02.696 --> 00:22:04.615
- [Tom Hanks] American
leaders understand

446
00:22:04.615 --> 00:22:07.910
the Battle of Okinawa
is a frightening preview

447
00:22:07.910 --> 00:22:12.122
of what to expect when Japan's
home islands are invaded.

448
00:22:12.122 --> 00:22:14.083
- Truman has a meeting
with General Marshall

449
00:22:14.083 --> 00:22:17.670
and the joint chiefs of
staff on Operation Downfall.

450
00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:20.798
the invasion of the
Japanese home islands.

451
00:22:20.798 --> 00:22:23.509
This plan goes back to
the Roosevelt presidency.

452
00:22:23.509 --> 00:22:24.885
Planning is going forward

453
00:22:26.136 --> 00:22:28.013
and the most interested
person at the meeting

454
00:22:28.013 --> 00:22:29.390
is George Marshall.

455
00:22:29.390 --> 00:22:31.892
He wants Truman
to sign off on it.

456
00:22:31.892 --> 00:22:34.854
- Operation Downfall will
be a two-pronged operation.

457
00:22:34.854 --> 00:22:37.064
the first called
Operation Olympic.

458
00:22:38.357 --> 00:22:39.567
The idea of Operation Olympic is

459
00:22:39.567 --> 00:22:42.152
to invade the southern
island of Kyushu

460
00:22:42.152 --> 00:22:43.654
and seize naval bases

461
00:22:43.654 --> 00:22:47.074
and air bases while pushing
Japanese forces north.

462
00:22:47.074 --> 00:22:49.243
The critical phase though
of Operation Downfall

463
00:22:49.243 --> 00:22:51.954
is what's code named
Operation Coronet.

464
00:22:51.954 --> 00:22:53.163
It's the invasion of Honshu.

465
00:22:53.163 --> 00:22:55.082
which is where
Tokyo itself sits.

466
00:22:55.082 --> 00:22:57.334
So this would inevitably end
in either the destruction

467
00:22:57.334 --> 00:22:59.169
or capture of the
Japanese capital.

468
00:22:59.169 --> 00:23:01.589
[dramatic music]

469
00:23:01.589 --> 00:23:03.424
- [Saul] Think about the scale
of Operation Downfall

470
00:23:03.424 --> 00:23:04.717
as a whole.

471
00:23:04.717 --> 00:23:07.803
5 million men involved.
up to a million British

472
00:23:07.803 --> 00:23:10.598
and Commonwealth troops.
4 million American troops.

473
00:23:10.598 --> 00:23:13.642
and this is gonna dwarf
D-Day in the logistics

474
00:23:13.642 --> 00:23:16.562
and the naval of forces
that are gonna be involved.

475
00:23:16.562 --> 00:23:18.856
Then the forces that
are actually gonna land

476
00:23:18.856 --> 00:23:21.442
and most importantly of all
is gonna dwarf the number

477
00:23:21.442 --> 00:23:22.693
of casualties.

478
00:23:22.693 --> 00:23:24.945
[soft music]

479
00:23:24.945 --> 00:23:26.614
- [Dan] If the allies had
to invade Japan.

480
00:23:26.614 --> 00:23:29.533
that would be the largest
amphibious assault

481
00:23:29.533 --> 00:23:30.868
ever undertaken.

482
00:23:31.911 --> 00:23:33.704
And when the Americans
were gonna bear

483
00:23:33.704 --> 00:23:35.080
almost the entire weight of it

484
00:23:36.874 --> 00:23:38.292
and the President
understood that better

485
00:23:38.292 --> 00:23:40.044
than most of the
American public did.

486
00:23:40.044 --> 00:23:42.546
[soft music continues]

487
00:23:42.546 --> 00:23:45.049
- [Saul] Truman specifically
says to his chiefs of staff.

488
00:23:45.049 --> 00:23:48.802
I don't want an Okinawa from
one end of Japan to the other.

489
00:23:48.802 --> 00:23:52.723
[soft music continues]

490
00:23:52.723 --> 00:23:56.602
- [Jon] So Truman in June
of '45 is looking

491
00:23:56.602 --> 00:24:00.481
at a brutal final
act against Japan.

492
00:24:00.481 --> 00:24:02.983
But it is very important
to Harry Truman

493
00:24:04.151 --> 00:24:06.445
that he always be
seen as the decider.

494
00:24:06.445 --> 00:24:07.488
The buck stops here.

495
00:24:09.281 --> 00:24:13.202
- Truman understands that this
war with Japan needs to end.

496
00:24:13.202 --> 00:24:15.454
the invasion looks horrible.

497
00:24:15.454 --> 00:24:18.082
But what's option B
in this situation?

498
00:24:19.249 --> 00:24:21.835
- Even at this late date
in June. there's no sense

499
00:24:21.835 --> 00:24:24.213
that the Manhattan
Project is going to work.

500
00:24:24.213 --> 00:24:27.216
They really don't know
if they have a bomb yet.

501
00:24:27.216 --> 00:24:30.636
- [Robert] And so Truman signs
off on Operation Downfall.

502
00:24:30.636 --> 00:24:33.430
the two big invasions of Japan.

503
00:24:33.430 --> 00:24:38.310
Stage one. Operation Olympic
is set for November 1st.

504
00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:39.436
The clock is ticking.

505
00:24:39.436 --> 00:24:42.356
[dramatic music]

506
00:24:43.649 --> 00:24:47.403
- [Noriko] The Japanese Army's
plan is to fight to the bitter

507
00:24:47.403 --> 00:24:53.283
end with a decisive and final
battle in the home islands.

508
00:24:55.035 --> 00:24:57.121
- [Christopher] For a while now.
the Japanese army had been

509
00:24:57.121 --> 00:24:58.831
planning what they call
Operation Ketsugo.

510
00:24:58.831 --> 00:25:00.791
which means operation decisive.

511
00:25:00.791 --> 00:25:02.876
It's a plan to defend Kyushu.

512
00:25:02.876 --> 00:25:04.878
They think they've got
perhaps a million men

513
00:25:04.878 --> 00:25:06.797
that they can put
into the field.

514
00:25:08.007 --> 00:25:10.718
They also want to put
civilians into the field.

515
00:25:12.011 --> 00:25:14.972
Their idea is that they will
make things so terrifying

516
00:25:14.972 --> 00:25:17.391
that it will stop
the Americans trying

517
00:25:17.391 --> 00:25:20.477
to do anything anywhere
else in the home islands.

518
00:25:20.477 --> 00:25:24.732
- [Yuki] Women and children
we're going through the military

519
00:25:24.732 --> 00:25:27.401
drills where everybody was
given a bamboo spear.

520
00:25:29.611 --> 00:25:33.032
They are essentially
mobilizing everybody

521
00:25:33.032 --> 00:25:38.037
older than 12 years old
to fight to the death.

522
00:25:39.038 --> 00:25:41.123
[crowd chanting
and dramatic music]

523
00:25:44.418 --> 00:25:46.545
[fast-paced music]

524
00:25:53.552 --> 00:25:55.012
- [Tom Hanks] In July
for the first time.

525
00:25:55.220 --> 00:25:57.848
President Truman meets with
both British Prime Minister

526
00:25:57.848 --> 00:26:01.518
Winston Churchill and
Soviet Premier Joseph Stalin

527
00:26:01.518 --> 00:26:05.522
at Potsdam. formerly the
home of Prussian Kings

528
00:26:05.522 --> 00:26:06.774
and the German Kaiser.

529
00:26:08.358 --> 00:26:09.777
- [A.J.] Europe is devastated.

530
00:26:09.777 --> 00:26:12.279
[soft music]

531
00:26:12.279 --> 00:26:14.531
It's a massive disaster zone.

532
00:26:16.241 --> 00:26:18.911
The point of the
Potsdam Conference is

533
00:26:18.911 --> 00:26:21.455
for these three governments
to come together

534
00:26:21.455 --> 00:26:24.374
and figure out how these
countries are gonna be rebuilt.

535
00:26:26.001 --> 00:26:29.963
- But Truman has his eyes on a
completely different problem.

536
00:26:29.963 --> 00:26:32.382
He huddles with
Secretary of War Stimson.

537
00:26:32.382 --> 00:26:34.593
and what are they talking about?

538
00:26:34.593 --> 00:26:36.887
They're talking
about the Pacific.

539
00:26:36.887 --> 00:26:39.973
[soft music]

540
00:26:39.973 --> 00:26:42.601
- [Tom Hanks] With Operation
Downfall months away.

541
00:26:42.601 --> 00:26:44.561
President Truman seeks assurance

542
00:26:44.561 --> 00:26:47.189
that the Soviets will
participate in the war

543
00:26:47.189 --> 00:26:50.067
against Japan as pledged.

544
00:26:50.067 --> 00:26:52.194
- [Craig] All this time. Japan

545
00:26:52.194 --> 00:26:54.238
and the Soviet Union
had been neutral.

546
00:26:54.238 --> 00:26:56.824
They had not been at
war with one another.

547
00:26:56.824 --> 00:26:59.993
- [Robert] Stalin promised
within 90 days of the end

548
00:26:59.993 --> 00:27:02.955
of the war in Europe. he'll
join the war against Japan.

549
00:27:02.955 --> 00:27:05.791
He promised that to
Roosevelt back at Yalta.

550
00:27:05.791 --> 00:27:07.584
So Truman hopes that's a go.

551
00:27:09.044 --> 00:27:10.921
- [Tom Hanks] But at the
start of the conference.

552
00:27:10.921 --> 00:27:15.509
Stimson receives a coded
cable from the War Department.

553
00:27:15.509 --> 00:27:19.930
- "Operated on this morning.
diagnosis not yet complete.

554
00:27:19.930 --> 00:27:22.474
but results seem satisfactory.

555
00:27:22.474 --> 00:27:24.309
Dr. Groves is pleased."

556
00:27:24.309 --> 00:27:25.686
[dramatic music]

557
00:27:25.811 --> 00:27:28.438
- [Robert] Truman now knew
that the atomic bomb

558
00:27:28.438 --> 00:27:30.440
had been detonated successfully.

559
00:27:30.440 --> 00:27:33.360
[dramatic music]

560
00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:35.946
- [A.J.] In this moment we
have this amazing window

561
00:27:35.946 --> 00:27:38.448
into Truman's thinking because
he was keeping a diary.

562
00:27:40.576 --> 00:27:43.662
Truman realizes this bomb
could be a short circuit

563
00:27:43.662 --> 00:27:45.038
to ending the war.

564
00:27:46.415 --> 00:27:51.545
- [Saul] When Truman hears that
the atomic bomb actually works.

565
00:27:51.545 --> 00:27:53.547
he knows they have to use it.

566
00:27:53.547 --> 00:27:56.174
but he wants to give the
Japanese one last chance

567
00:27:56.174 --> 00:27:57.926
to surrender before he does.

568
00:27:57.926 --> 00:27:59.678
[dramatic music]

569
00:27:59.678 --> 00:28:01.722
- [Tom Hanks] President
Truman. along with Churchill

570
00:28:01.722 --> 00:28:05.684
and Chiang Kai-shek. the leader
of the Republic of China.

571
00:28:05.684 --> 00:28:09.563
issue the Potsdam Declaration.
a warning to Japan

572
00:28:09.563 --> 00:28:12.441
of what awaits if they don't
surrender unconditionally.

573
00:28:14.026 --> 00:28:17.321
But Stimson suggests they
consider allowing Hirohito

574
00:28:17.321 --> 00:28:18.322
to keep his throne.

575
00:28:19.531 --> 00:28:22.200
- [Saul] Stimson understands
the constitutional

576
00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:24.912
and cultural position
that the emperor plays

577
00:28:24.912 --> 00:28:27.956
in Japanese life. this
kind of demigod status.

578
00:28:27.956 --> 00:28:31.293
And so he insists on inserting
into the declaration.

579
00:28:31.293 --> 00:28:34.212
assurance that they won't try
the emperor for war crimes.

580
00:28:34.212 --> 00:28:37.758
But beyond that. it will
be unconditional surrender

581
00:28:37.758 --> 00:28:39.843
and no other terms
will be acceptable.

582
00:28:40.886 --> 00:28:43.597
- [A.J.] Also in this
document is wordage

583
00:28:43.597 --> 00:28:46.016
that does not mention
the atomic bomb.

584
00:28:46.016 --> 00:28:49.645
but it does say that if
unconditional surrender

585
00:28:49.645 --> 00:28:53.732
is not coming. the destruction
was going to be historic

586
00:28:53.732 --> 00:28:55.067
and massive.

587
00:28:55.067 --> 00:28:57.945
[dramatic music]

588
00:28:59.404 --> 00:29:01.323
- [Tom Hanks] The Office
of War Information

589
00:29:01.323 --> 00:29:03.951
releases the Potsdam Declaration

590
00:29:05.285 --> 00:29:08.622
and the Japanese receive it
via radio from San Francisco.

591
00:29:10.165 --> 00:29:14.586
- [Noriko] Prime Minister Suzuki
and Emperor Hirohito

592
00:29:14.586 --> 00:29:19.549
are the opinion that
the Potsdam Declaration

593
00:29:20.384 --> 00:29:24.304
is not really vindictive.

594
00:29:24.304 --> 00:29:28.600
They see some hope.

595
00:29:29.685 --> 00:29:32.104
- [Yuki] Emperor Hirohito
himself at this point.

596
00:29:32.104 --> 00:29:34.481
he is open to accepting it.

597
00:29:34.481 --> 00:29:39.361
but he understands his
role as an emperor.

598
00:29:39.361 --> 00:29:42.280
There are only certain
times for him to weigh in.

599
00:29:43.490 --> 00:29:44.866
- [Saul] The emperor and
the Peace Party

600
00:29:44.866 --> 00:29:46.326
are in an impossible
position here.

601
00:29:46.326 --> 00:29:49.413
I mean. if they respond
positively to the ultimatum.

602
00:29:49.413 --> 00:29:51.081
the military might
turn against them.

603
00:29:51.081 --> 00:29:53.792
but if they completely
ignore it. there's a danger

604
00:29:53.792 --> 00:29:55.252
that the Americans are
actually gonna carry out

605
00:29:55.252 --> 00:29:56.294
what they suggest.

606
00:29:58.171 --> 00:30:00.924
- [Tom Hanks] Two days
later. Japan's Prime Minister

607
00:30:00.924 --> 00:30:04.386
Kantaro Suzuki holds
a press conference.

608
00:30:05.595 --> 00:30:08.890
- [Yuki] When press asks about
Potsdam Declaration.

609
00:30:08.890 --> 00:30:11.643
Suzuki uses the word mokusatsu.

610
00:30:12.853 --> 00:30:16.523
The best translation
for that is no comment

611
00:30:16.523 --> 00:30:20.110
or I'm not going to
respond to that question.

612
00:30:21.361 --> 00:30:24.322
However. the word
mokusatsu can also mean

613
00:30:24.322 --> 00:30:26.116
we're going to ignore it.

614
00:30:26.116 --> 00:30:27.367
- [Christopher] And I think the
Japanese media.

615
00:30:27.367 --> 00:30:29.202
they're very quick
to interpret it

616
00:30:29.202 --> 00:30:31.121
in the most hostile
and negative way.

617
00:30:32.372 --> 00:30:34.082
And then the American
media pick it up.

618
00:30:34.082 --> 00:30:35.375
And then that becomes the view

619
00:30:35.375 --> 00:30:38.045
of the American
administration as well

620
00:30:38.045 --> 00:30:41.757
that the Japanese are rejecting
the Potsdam Declaration.

621
00:30:41.757 --> 00:30:43.800
- [A.J.] And to Truman. the
Japanese response is

622
00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:45.844
plain and clear.

623
00:30:45.844 --> 00:30:49.139
He believes that the Japanese
told him to go to hell.

624
00:30:52.392 --> 00:30:54.227
[dramatic music]

625
00:30:55.520 --> 00:30:58.065
- [Tom Hanks] By early 1945.

626
00:30:58.065 --> 00:31:00.484
Tinian Island in the Marianas

627
00:31:00.484 --> 00:31:03.236
is the largest
air base on Earth.

628
00:31:05.197 --> 00:31:08.992
Hundreds of B-29s have taken
off from its multiple runways

629
00:31:08.992 --> 00:31:10.285
to bomb Japan.

630
00:31:10.285 --> 00:31:13.163
[dramatic music]

631
00:31:17.209 --> 00:31:20.962
- But there's a very
mysterious restricted area

632
00:31:20.962 --> 00:31:23.090
at one part of the base.

633
00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:25.842
No one really knows
what's going on up there.

634
00:31:25.842 --> 00:31:30.639
It belongs to some unit called
the 509th Composite Group.

635
00:31:30.639 --> 00:31:33.642
These are the preparations
for the arrival

636
00:31:33.642 --> 00:31:35.310
of the atomic bomb.

637
00:31:35.310 --> 00:31:37.187
[dramatic music]

638
00:31:37.187 --> 00:31:39.231
- [Tom Hanks] Components
for the atomic bombs

639
00:31:39.231 --> 00:31:40.482
are delivered in late July

640
00:31:41.691 --> 00:31:44.611
and carefully assembled
over the following days.

641
00:31:44.611 --> 00:31:46.613
[dramatic music continues]

642
00:31:46.613 --> 00:31:48.907
- [A.J.] So the Manhattan
Project was able

643
00:31:48.907 --> 00:31:52.994
to produce enough uranium and
plutonium for three bombs.

644
00:31:52.994 --> 00:31:54.788
And after the Trinity test.

645
00:31:54.788 --> 00:31:58.250
the Americans only had
two bombs in the arsenal.

646
00:31:58.250 --> 00:32:01.044
- [Robert] Truman makes the
decision to drop the bomb.

647
00:32:01.044 --> 00:32:03.630
He legitimately wants
to save US casualties

648
00:32:03.630 --> 00:32:06.133
and bring this war
to an end rapidly.

649
00:32:06.133 --> 00:32:07.884
At the same time. he wants

650
00:32:07.884 --> 00:32:10.387
to assert his
authority as president.

651
00:32:10.387 --> 00:32:13.265
[dramatic music]

652
00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:19.563
- [Jon] It is one of the most
consequential decisions

653
00:32:19.563 --> 00:32:24.484
that any nation has ever made.

654
00:32:26.570 --> 00:32:30.323
and no one knows what
was gonna happen.

655
00:32:32.033 --> 00:32:36.121
But President Truman did
understand that this was

656
00:32:36.121 --> 00:32:38.415
of a different scale and scope.

657
00:32:38.415 --> 00:32:42.043
He compares it to the
biblical flood in his diary.

658
00:32:42.043 --> 00:32:45.922
"As the world was once
destroyed by water for Noah.

659
00:32:47.591 --> 00:32:50.051
perhaps now it'll be destroyed
by fire and destruction."

660
00:32:50.051 --> 00:32:52.929
[dramatic music]

661
00:32:54.723 --> 00:32:57.601
- [Tom Hanks] By design. the
target of the first atomic bomb

662
00:32:57.601 --> 00:33:00.645
is a city relatively
unscathed by the war.

663
00:33:02.147 --> 00:33:05.358
American military
leaders want the Japanese

664
00:33:05.358 --> 00:33:08.987
to understand the
awful destructive power

665
00:33:08.987 --> 00:33:10.614
of this new weapon.

666
00:33:10.614 --> 00:33:13.408
- That morning in Hiroshima
is an ordinary morning.

667
00:33:13.408 --> 00:33:15.202
People are going
about their business.

668
00:33:15.202 --> 00:33:17.871
Some people are going into
the factories to work.

669
00:33:17.871 --> 00:33:20.665
They're still wondering why
they've not yet been bombed

670
00:33:20.665 --> 00:33:23.668
and they're expecting
it more or less any day.

671
00:33:23.668 --> 00:33:26.963
And then people see
this light in the sky.

672
00:33:26.963 --> 00:33:29.716
sun shining off a
single aircraft.

673
00:33:30.634 --> 00:33:33.386
[dramatic music]

674
00:33:53.490 --> 00:33:56.534
[airplane whirring]

675
00:34:02.666 --> 00:34:04.251
[soft music]

676
00:34:04.251 --> 00:34:08.505
- [Yuki] The first thing is
the flashing light

677
00:34:08.505 --> 00:34:10.172
as if sun explodes.

678
00:34:11.174 --> 00:34:15.053
then a noise and a shock.

679
00:34:15.053 --> 00:34:18.514
[soft music]

680
00:34:18.514 --> 00:34:23.520
The people who were near
the epicenter evaporate.

681
00:34:24.062 --> 00:34:25.605
[soft music]

682
00:34:25.813 --> 00:34:28.525
- [Christopher] The casualties
mount until you get between

683
00:34:28.525 --> 00:34:31.360
90.000 and 160.000
people who've died.

684
00:34:33.321 --> 00:34:36.824
The dominant image that
survivors give of Hiroshima

685
00:34:36.824 --> 00:34:39.244
is of one of the Buddhist hells.

686
00:34:39.244 --> 00:34:41.913
[soft music]

687
00:34:45.125 --> 00:34:47.252
- [A.J.] One thing that's really
difficult for historians to jive

688
00:34:47.252 --> 00:34:51.630
with is this idea that Truman
has chosen military targets

689
00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:53.466
and he writes even in
his diary. the words.

690
00:34:53.466 --> 00:34:55.135
"And not women and children."

691
00:34:56.136 --> 00:34:58.096
We'll never really know.

692
00:34:58.096 --> 00:35:01.641
Did he really feel that
this was a military target

693
00:35:02.684 --> 00:35:04.269
or was he writing
that in his diary

694
00:35:04.269 --> 00:35:06.104
understanding that history

695
00:35:06.104 --> 00:35:08.648
for generations was
gonna judge him for this.

696
00:35:08.648 --> 00:35:11.318
[soft music]

697
00:35:14.029 --> 00:35:16.656
- [Robert] Truman is at sea
coming home from Potsdam

698
00:35:16.656 --> 00:35:18.992
when he received the news.

699
00:35:18.992 --> 00:35:21.202
Later that same day.
he goes on the airwaves

700
00:35:21.202 --> 00:35:22.787
to address the American people

701
00:35:22.787 --> 00:35:24.873
and to tell them what
has just happened.

702
00:35:24.873 --> 00:35:28.918
He says it's a new type of
bomb that America has used it

703
00:35:28.918 --> 00:35:32.547
and will continue to use
it until Japan surrenders.

704
00:35:32.547 --> 00:35:35.425
- If they do not now
accept our terms.

705
00:35:35.425 --> 00:35:38.511
they may expect a rain
of ruin from the air

706
00:35:38.511 --> 00:35:41.306
the like of which has never
been seen on this earth.

707
00:35:41.306 --> 00:35:44.184
[dramatic music]

708
00:35:48.605 --> 00:35:51.024
- [Tom Hanks] Even with
Hiroshima in ruins.

709
00:35:51.024 --> 00:35:53.568
Japanese leaders
do not surrender.

710
00:35:53.568 --> 00:35:57.322
- [Yuki] The peace camp pushed
for immediate acceptance

711
00:35:57.322 --> 00:35:59.324
of the Potsdam Declaration.

712
00:35:59.324 --> 00:36:03.495
but the military side insists
on continuing the war.

713
00:36:03.495 --> 00:36:05.163
The reasoning is

714
00:36:05.163 --> 00:36:08.208
that may be the only
atomic bomb that they had.

715
00:36:08.208 --> 00:36:10.669
So they're still
at the stalemate.

716
00:36:12.045 --> 00:36:14.839
And then the Soviet Union
declares war in Japan.

717
00:36:14.839 --> 00:36:17.717
[dramatic music]

718
00:36:21.763 --> 00:36:25.517
- [Tom Hanks] Just as Stalin
promised exactly 90 days

719
00:36:25.517 --> 00:36:27.394
after the surrender of Germany.

720
00:36:29.229 --> 00:36:32.190
one and a half
million Soviet troops

721
00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:36.194
pour across the border into
Japanese occupied Manchuria.

722
00:36:37.404 --> 00:36:39.197
- [Christopher] The Japanese
aren't aware that for months

723
00:36:39.197 --> 00:36:41.199
the Soviets have
masked their armor

724
00:36:41.199 --> 00:36:43.535
on the borders with Manchuria.

725
00:36:43.535 --> 00:36:46.413
But now the Russians are
rolling into Manchuria

726
00:36:46.413 --> 00:36:49.040
and they're rolling
over the Japanese.

727
00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:50.709
And now you've got the prospect

728
00:36:50.709 --> 00:36:53.753
of the Soviets not just making
their way into Manchuria.

729
00:36:53.753 --> 00:36:56.464
but possibly across to
the Japanese home islands.

730
00:36:56.464 --> 00:36:57.674
[dramatic music]

731
00:36:57.674 --> 00:36:59.884
For Japan. it's a
complete disaster.

732
00:37:03.346 --> 00:37:05.598
[dramatic music]

733
00:37:05.807 --> 00:37:08.143
- [Tom Hanks] On the same
day the Red Army moves

734
00:37:08.143 --> 00:37:12.480
into Manchuria. the B-29s
appear over Nagasaki.

735
00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:16.192
into Manchuria. the B-29s
appear over Nagasaki.

736
00:37:17.193 --> 00:37:19.237
One carries an atomic bomb.

737
00:37:20.405 --> 00:37:23.533
- [Dr. Rebecca] The US has just
one more atom bomb left.

738
00:37:25.076 --> 00:37:28.121
But the whole idea is to
give Japan the impression

739
00:37:28.121 --> 00:37:30.749
that the US has
an unlimited stock

740
00:37:32.083 --> 00:37:34.252
and that if Japan
won't surrender.

741
00:37:34.252 --> 00:37:38.882
the US will continue to
unleash atom bombs on Japan.

742
00:37:38.882 --> 00:37:41.760
[dramatic music]

743
00:37:42.761 --> 00:37:45.722
- [Tom Hanks] Between
60 and 70.000 people

744
00:37:45.722 --> 00:37:47.515
are killed instantly.

745
00:37:47.515 --> 00:37:50.393
[dramatic music]

746
00:37:59.235 --> 00:38:02.322
- [Saul] Prime Minister Suzuki
convenes the war council

747
00:38:02.322 --> 00:38:03.531
and the discussion is.

748
00:38:03.531 --> 00:38:06.493
are we gonna accept the
Potsdam Declaration?

749
00:38:08.119 --> 00:38:10.205
And the answer from
the military is no.

750
00:38:10.205 --> 00:38:13.374
[dramatic music]

751
00:38:13.374 --> 00:38:15.126
- [Christopher] You've had two
atomic bombs.

752
00:38:15.126 --> 00:38:17.962
You've had the Soviet
invasion of Manchuria.

753
00:38:17.962 --> 00:38:21.007
and yet the Supreme War
Council is still split.

754
00:38:21.007 --> 00:38:23.051
The war faction. the military.

755
00:38:23.051 --> 00:38:24.928
they're absolutely not
willing to consider

756
00:38:24.928 --> 00:38:26.721
unconditional surrender.

757
00:38:26.721 --> 00:38:29.516
- [Yuki] That's when Prime
Minister Suzuki

758
00:38:29.516 --> 00:38:32.602
makes the very important move

759
00:38:32.602 --> 00:38:36.481
saying that we need
to ask the emperor.

760
00:38:37.649 --> 00:38:39.192
- [Christopher] This is a
standout moment.

761
00:38:39.192 --> 00:38:41.402
This is something you
would never normally do.

762
00:38:41.402 --> 00:38:43.988
There's a sense
Hirohito is semi divine

763
00:38:43.988 --> 00:38:47.242
and shouldn't be troubled with
the decision making process.

764
00:38:48.326 --> 00:38:50.036
It's a really
extraordinary step.

765
00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:53.206
- [Bradley] But Hirohito. I
think. sees the gravity

766
00:38:53.206 --> 00:38:54.999
of the situation.

767
00:38:54.999 --> 00:38:57.502
He sees that this is a moment
where it's his responsibility

768
00:38:57.502 --> 00:38:59.796
to step up as the
leader effectively.

769
00:39:01.047 --> 00:39:03.633
- [Noriko] And he says.

770
00:39:03.633 --> 00:39:08.638
"We must bear the unbearable
and suffer the insufferable.

771
00:39:09.681 --> 00:39:13.393
We will end the war
and then move on."

772
00:39:15.103 --> 00:39:17.480
[soft music]

773
00:39:24.946 --> 00:39:27.824
- I have received this
afternoon a message

774
00:39:27.824 --> 00:39:30.285
from the Japanese government.

775
00:39:30.285 --> 00:39:33.037
I deem this reply.
a full acceptance

776
00:39:33.037 --> 00:39:35.707
of the Potsdam Declaration.

777
00:39:35.707 --> 00:39:37.584
which specifies
the unconditional

778
00:39:37.584 --> 00:39:39.168
surrender of Japan.

779
00:39:41.129 --> 00:39:42.714
- And people go nuts.

780
00:39:42.714 --> 00:39:44.632
Tens of thousands
of people gather

781
00:39:44.632 --> 00:39:46.426
at the gate of the White House.

782
00:39:47.802 --> 00:39:49.804
- [Tom Hanks] The world
has been holding its breath

783
00:39:49.804 --> 00:39:51.431
since the war began.

784
00:39:52.724 --> 00:39:57.520
Now it is over and there
is great celebration.

785
00:39:59.230 --> 00:40:01.357
- [Robert] One of the crucial
factors in ending this war is

786
00:40:01.357 --> 00:40:04.152
that you have Harry Truman.
an accidental president

787
00:40:04.152 --> 00:40:06.362
who had to be briefed
on the wartime situation

788
00:40:06.362 --> 00:40:08.072
when he enters the Oval Office.

789
00:40:09.449 --> 00:40:13.953
And Hirohito. who has never
really made political decisions.

790
00:40:15.163 --> 00:40:17.999
They assert themselves and
bring the war to an end

791
00:40:17.999 --> 00:40:19.709
in August of 1945.

792
00:40:19.709 --> 00:40:22.754
[dramatic music]

793
00:40:22.754 --> 00:40:26.841
- [Tom Hanks] Batan.
Guadalcanal. Iwo Jima.

794
00:40:27.884 --> 00:40:32.347
Okinawa. once obscure
points on a map.

795
00:40:32.347 --> 00:40:35.975
now each place
has a new meaning.

796
00:40:35.975 --> 00:40:38.561
[soft music]

797
00:40:42.190 --> 00:40:47.153
In Japan on August 15th. Emperor
Hirohito addresses a nation

798
00:40:48.237 --> 00:40:49.989
that has never surrendered.

799
00:40:51.366 --> 00:40:54.452
- [Robert] Most Japanese had
never heard the emperor's voice

800
00:40:54.452 --> 00:40:57.747
before. but the Jewel Voice
Broadcast as it became known.

801
00:40:57.747 --> 00:41:02.210
is essential to getting most
Japanese used to the idea

802
00:41:02.210 --> 00:41:05.838
of laying their arms
and admitting surrender.

803
00:41:05.838 --> 00:41:10.510
[Hirohito speaking
in foreign language]

804
00:41:21.104 --> 00:41:23.523
- [Dan] When the Japanese hear
that the war is over.

805
00:41:23.523 --> 00:41:26.025
there has to be a
combination of feelings.

806
00:41:26.025 --> 00:41:28.403
We sacrificed all these
people. all this money

807
00:41:28.403 --> 00:41:31.864
and these cities of
Japan. and we lost.

808
00:41:32.991 --> 00:41:35.201
- [Yuki] Shock. sadness.

809
00:41:37.036 --> 00:41:40.790
but also this incredible
sense of grief

810
00:41:40.790 --> 00:41:42.834
that we let the emperor down.

811
00:41:44.043 --> 00:41:45.962
Our lack of effort
let the emperor down.

812
00:41:47.672 --> 00:41:50.842
- [Craig] The Japanese accepted
the idea of surrender.

813
00:41:50.842 --> 00:41:52.719
The allies accepted the idea

814
00:41:52.719 --> 00:41:55.722
of Hirohito remaining
on the Japanese throne.

815
00:41:55.722 --> 00:41:57.473
and that was the end of the war.

816
00:41:58.683 --> 00:42:01.894
- [Tom Hanks] September 2nd.
1945 on the deck

817
00:42:01.894 --> 00:42:05.565
of the USS Missouri
anchored in Tokyo Bay.

818
00:42:05.565 --> 00:42:08.651
the Japanese sign the
instrument of surrender.

819
00:42:09.986 --> 00:42:12.363
the largest conflict
in world history closes

820
00:42:12.363 --> 00:42:14.574
with a signature and a speech.

821
00:42:15.992 --> 00:42:18.244
Douglas MacArthur
marks the occasion.

822
00:42:18.244 --> 00:42:19.704
"It is my earnest hope

823
00:42:19.704 --> 00:42:21.998
and indeed the hope
of all mankind.

824
00:42:21.998 --> 00:42:23.750
that from this solemn occasion.

825
00:42:23.750 --> 00:42:27.920
a better world shall emerge
out of the blood and carnage

826
00:42:27.920 --> 00:42:29.047
of the past."
